{
  "_meta": {
    "schema_version": 1,
    "description": "Data-driven registry of Windows system capabilities JARVIS can reach without generic UI automation: 'panes' deep-link straight into a specific Settings page (ms-settings: URI scheme), 'queries' run a single built-in, read-only Windows cmdlet and return its real output. Adding a new capability means adding a row here, not writing a new Python module per app — see actions/system_shortcuts.py.",
    "panes_source": "https://github.com/MicrosoftDocs/windows-dev-docs/blob/docs/hub/apps/develop/launch/launch-settings.md (official Microsoft docs repo — ms-settings: URI reference). Only long-standing, stable-since-Windows-10 pages were kept; newer/niche pages (e.g. Dynamic Lighting, Presence sensing) were deliberately left out for cross-build reliability. Microsoft does not publish a version-pinned guarantee for these URIs, so a pane that 404s on a given build should degrade to the OS-level generic open, never crash.",
    "queries_source": "Built-in Windows PowerShell cmdlets only (Get-PnpDevice, Get-CimInstance, Get-NetIPConfiguration, Get-Printer, Get-NetFirewallProfile, netsh) — no third-party CLI tools, no extra pip/OS dependency. installed_apps deliberately reads the Uninstall registry key instead of Get-CimInstance Win32_Product, which is a well-known trap: it silently triggers an MSI reconfigure/repair pass on every installed package and can be slow or disruptive."
  },
